        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/421">

	<title>AI, Vol. 7, Pages 421: Impact of Class Imbalance Correction on Biological Feature Selection in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma Methylation Data</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/421</link>
	<description>Class imbalance is common in biomedical machine learning, yet imbalance-correction strategies are typically evaluated using predictive performance rather than their effects on biological interpretation. We evaluated six resampling methods: Random Oversampling (ROS), Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE), Adaptive Synthetic Sampling (ADASYN), Random Undersampling (RUS), ClusterCentroids, and AllKNN (All K-Nearest Neighbors), together with a no-resampling baseline, using DNA methylation data from The Cancer Genome Atlas Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC) cohort. The analysis included 485,578 CpG markers of which 406,807 were retained after quality-control filtering. A continuous feature-ranking framework merging Random Forest importance and ANOVA F-statistics was applied across repeated runs. Biological relevance was evaluated through hypergeometric enrichment against a curated PDAC gene set and preranked Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) using 309 Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) pathways. All seven analysis strategies produced significant enrichment for literature-supported pancreatic cancer-associated genes, with hypergeometric p-values ranging from 3.34&amp;amp;times;10&amp;amp;minus;16 to 1.28&amp;amp;times;10&amp;amp;minus;7. SMOTE recovered the largest absolute number of PDAC-associated genes (141 of 13,827 selected genes), whereas RUS achieved the greatest fold enrichment (2.17-fold; 88 observed versus 40.61 expected). Notably, the no-resampling baseline remained highly competitive, recovering 112 PDAC-associated genes with 1.86-fold enrichment and a p-value of 5.27&amp;amp;times;10&amp;amp;minus;14, indicating that resampling was not necessary to recover biologically relevant features under the evaluated feature-ranking framework. Across methods, 169&amp;amp;ndash;234 of the 309 tested KEGG pathways were significant at false discovery rate &amp;amp;le;0.05. ClusterCentroids produced the largest number of significant pathways (234), followed by the no-resampling baseline (225) and RUS (223). Seven to nine predefined PDAC-focused pathways were recovered per method, with RUS and ClusterCentroids recovering all nine. Core pathways consistently identified across all methods included MAPK, PI3K&amp;amp;ndash;Akt, TGF-&amp;amp;beta;, Notch, Hedgehog, focal adhesion, and cell-cycle signaling. These findings show that resampling strategies produce distinct trade-offs between biomarker coverage, biological specificity, and pathway breadth, supporting biologically informed evaluation rather than selection of a single universally optimal imbalance-correction method.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-13</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>AI, Vol. 7, Pages 421: Impact of Class Imbalance Correction on Biological Feature Selection in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma Methylation Data</b></p>
	<p>AI <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/421">doi: 10.3390/ai7100421</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Paige Keller
		Zachary Caterer
		Kaia Hansen
		Nijhum Paul
		Amanda Warren
		Samuel T. Morehead
		Rick J. Jansen
		Rahul Gomes
		</p>
	<p>Class imbalance is common in biomedical machine learning, yet imbalance-correction strategies are typically evaluated using predictive performance rather than their effects on biological interpretation. We evaluated six resampling methods: Random Oversampling (ROS), Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE), Adaptive Synthetic Sampling (ADASYN), Random Undersampling (RUS), ClusterCentroids, and AllKNN (All K-Nearest Neighbors), together with a no-resampling baseline, using DNA methylation data from The Cancer Genome Atlas Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC) cohort. The analysis included 485,578 CpG markers of which 406,807 were retained after quality-control filtering. A continuous feature-ranking framework merging Random Forest importance and ANOVA F-statistics was applied across repeated runs. Biological relevance was evaluated through hypergeometric enrichment against a curated PDAC gene set and preranked Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) using 309 Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) pathways. All seven analysis strategies produced significant enrichment for literature-supported pancreatic cancer-associated genes, with hypergeometric p-values ranging from 3.34&amp;amp;times;10&amp;amp;minus;16 to 1.28&amp;amp;times;10&amp;amp;minus;7. SMOTE recovered the largest absolute number of PDAC-associated genes (141 of 13,827 selected genes), whereas RUS achieved the greatest fold enrichment (2.17-fold; 88 observed versus 40.61 expected). Notably, the no-resampling baseline remained highly competitive, recovering 112 PDAC-associated genes with 1.86-fold enrichment and a p-value of 5.27&amp;amp;times;10&amp;amp;minus;14, indicating that resampling was not necessary to recover biologically relevant features under the evaluated feature-ranking framework. Across methods, 169&amp;amp;ndash;234 of the 309 tested KEGG pathways were significant at false discovery rate &amp;amp;le;0.05. ClusterCentroids produced the largest number of significant pathways (234), followed by the no-resampling baseline (225) and RUS (223). Seven to nine predefined PDAC-focused pathways were recovered per method, with RUS and ClusterCentroids recovering all nine. Core pathways consistently identified across all methods included MAPK, PI3K&amp;amp;ndash;Akt, TGF-&amp;amp;beta;, Notch, Hedgehog, focal adhesion, and cell-cycle signaling. These findings show that resampling strategies produce distinct trade-offs between biomarker coverage, biological specificity, and pathway breadth, supporting biologically informed evaluation rather than selection of a single universally optimal imbalance-correction method.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Impact of Class Imbalance Correction on Biological Feature Selection in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma Methylation Data</dc:title>
