        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/420">

	<title>AI, Vol. 7, Pages 420: AI-Generated Text Detection via Structural and Semantic Entropy Optimization with Adversarial Learning</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/420</link>
	<description>AI-generated text detectors often achieve high in-domain accuracy but remain sensitive to changes in domain, generator, and writing style. This limitation partly arises because conventional detectors rely predominantly on latent language-model representations and fixed decision boundaries, without explicitly characterizing sample-level structural and semantic variation. We introduce S2E-AL, an end-to-end score-aware structure&amp;amp;ndash;semantic entropy adversarial learning framework for AI-generated text detection. S2E-AL encodes local dependency, global contextual, and semantic entropy features into a structure&amp;amp;ndash;semantic representation. A gradient-reversal source classifier discourages this representation from retaining source-specific information, while a score-aware threshold estimator conditions on both the entropy representation and a detached base-detector logit to produce a sample-specific logit adjustment. The adjusted logit is used to estimate the final probability that a text is AI-generated. Across HC3, HC3_Plus, LLMTrace, RAID, and XSum, the score-aware model achieved a macro Accuracy of 90.90% and a macro F1-score of 91.42%. Its performance remained lower on XSum, indicating that substantial domain and stylistic shifts remain challenging. These results suggest that jointly modeling structure&amp;amp;ndash;semantic variation and detector confidence can improve the overall performance&amp;amp;ndash;stability trade-off of AI-generated text detection across heterogeneous test distributions.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-12</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>AI, Vol. 7, Pages 420: AI-Generated Text Detection via Structural and Semantic Entropy Optimization with Adversarial Learning</b></p>
	<p>AI <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/420">doi: 10.3390/ai7100420</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Yue Teng
		Shaomeng Zhang
		Gang Ma
		Xiaonan Sun
		Jikang Zhang
		Yi Liu
		Yujian Wang
		Hua Yue
		</p>
	<p>AI-generated text detectors often achieve high in-domain accuracy but remain sensitive to changes in domain, generator, and writing style. This limitation partly arises because conventional detectors rely predominantly on latent language-model representations and fixed decision boundaries, without explicitly characterizing sample-level structural and semantic variation. We introduce S2E-AL, an end-to-end score-aware structure&amp;amp;ndash;semantic entropy adversarial learning framework for AI-generated text detection. S2E-AL encodes local dependency, global contextual, and semantic entropy features into a structure&amp;amp;ndash;semantic representation. A gradient-reversal source classifier discourages this representation from retaining source-specific information, while a score-aware threshold estimator conditions on both the entropy representation and a detached base-detector logit to produce a sample-specific logit adjustment. The adjusted logit is used to estimate the final probability that a text is AI-generated. Across HC3, HC3_Plus, LLMTrace, RAID, and XSum, the score-aware model achieved a macro Accuracy of 90.90% and a macro F1-score of 91.42%. Its performance remained lower on XSum, indicating that substantial domain and stylistic shifts remain challenging. These results suggest that jointly modeling structure&amp;amp;ndash;semantic variation and detector confidence can improve the overall performance&amp;amp;ndash;stability trade-off of AI-generated text detection across heterogeneous test distributions.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>AI-Generated Text Detection via Structural and Semantic Entropy Optimization with Adversarial Learning</dc:title>
