        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/414">

	<title>AI, Vol. 7, Pages 414: Explicit Multi-Scale Feature Learning Through a Human Visual System-Inspired Coarse&amp;ndash;Medium&amp;ndash;Fine Network for Maize Leaf Disease Classification</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/414</link>
	<description>Accurately recognizing maize leaf diseases requires analyzing visual information expressed across multiple spatial scales. This includes the leaf&amp;amp;rsquo;s overall appearance, intermediate-scale texture structures, and localized lesion patterns. This study proposes a coarse&amp;amp;ndash;medium&amp;amp;ndash;fine (CMF) architecture inspired by the human visual system that uses a convolutional neural network to classify maize leaf images into four categories: blight, common rust, gray leaf spot, and healthy. The CMF architecture contains three parallel convolutional branches that learn complementary coarse, medium, and fine representations. The feature maps are integrated through a weighted fusion strategy and processed by a fully connected classifier. The model was trained from scratch using a balanced subset of 2000 original images selected from the Corn or Maize Leaf Disease Dataset. The experimental protocol used 1600 images for training and 400 for testing. Augmentation was applied to the training set only; the testing set remained unchanged. The Coarse, Medium, and Fine branches achieved accuracies of 87.95&amp;amp;plusmn;0.58, 89.85&amp;amp;plusmn;0.51, and 95.10&amp;amp;plusmn;0.39, respectively. A fixed held-out test partition was used to evaluate the three individual branches and the complete CMF model. Five balanced random subsets of 300 images (75 images per class) were employed for this evaluation. The resulting performance was summarized using the mean and standard deviation across the five test-subset evaluations. The complete CMF model achieved 99.25&amp;amp;plusmn;0.27 accuracy, as well as macro-averaged precision, recall, and F1-score values of 99.27&amp;amp;plusmn;0.26, 99.25&amp;amp;plusmn;0.27, and 99.24&amp;amp;plusmn;0.26, respectively. Under a common, repeated, random subsampling evaluation protocol applied to the three individual branches and the complete CMF model, the fused model improved accuracy by 4.15 percentage points relative to the Fine branch. Blight and Healthy achieved recall values of 100.00&amp;amp;plusmn;0.00, while Gray Leaf Spot and Common Rust achieved recall values of 99.00&amp;amp;plusmn;0.15% and 98&amp;amp;plusmn;0.39%, respectively. The branch-level and fused-model results support the complementary role of the three spatial representations under the evaluated experimental conditions. The proposed CMF architecture provides an explicit, multiscale learning framework for image-based maize leaf disease classification and motivates further evaluation under practical agricultural conditions.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-08</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>AI, Vol. 7, Pages 414: Explicit Multi-Scale Feature Learning Through a Human Visual System-Inspired Coarse&amp;ndash;Medium&amp;ndash;Fine Network for Maize Leaf Disease Classification</b></p>
	<p>AI <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/414">doi: 10.3390/ai7100414</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Claudia Sifuentes-Gallardo
		José Antonio Lara-Chávez
		Carlos Avilés-Cruz
		José Félix Serrano-Talamantes
		Miriam Aguilar-Sánchez
		Luis Carlos Reveles-Gómez
		Héctor Antonio Durán-Muñoz
		</p>
	<p>Accurately recognizing maize leaf diseases requires analyzing visual information expressed across multiple spatial scales. This includes the leaf&amp;amp;rsquo;s overall appearance, intermediate-scale texture structures, and localized lesion patterns. This study proposes a coarse&amp;amp;ndash;medium&amp;amp;ndash;fine (CMF) architecture inspired by the human visual system that uses a convolutional neural network to classify maize leaf images into four categories: blight, common rust, gray leaf spot, and healthy. The CMF architecture contains three parallel convolutional branches that learn complementary coarse, medium, and fine representations. The feature maps are integrated through a weighted fusion strategy and processed by a fully connected classifier. The model was trained from scratch using a balanced subset of 2000 original images selected from the Corn or Maize Leaf Disease Dataset. The experimental protocol used 1600 images for training and 400 for testing. Augmentation was applied to the training set only; the testing set remained unchanged. The Coarse, Medium, and Fine branches achieved accuracies of 87.95&amp;amp;plusmn;0.58, 89.85&amp;amp;plusmn;0.51, and 95.10&amp;amp;plusmn;0.39, respectively. A fixed held-out test partition was used to evaluate the three individual branches and the complete CMF model. Five balanced random subsets of 300 images (75 images per class) were employed for this evaluation. The resulting performance was summarized using the mean and standard deviation across the five test-subset evaluations. The complete CMF model achieved 99.25&amp;amp;plusmn;0.27 accuracy, as well as macro-averaged precision, recall, and F1-score values of 99.27&amp;amp;plusmn;0.26, 99.25&amp;amp;plusmn;0.27, and 99.24&amp;amp;plusmn;0.26, respectively. Under a common, repeated, random subsampling evaluation protocol applied to the three individual branches and the complete CMF model, the fused model improved accuracy by 4.15 percentage points relative to the Fine branch. Blight and Healthy achieved recall values of 100.00&amp;amp;plusmn;0.00, while Gray Leaf Spot and Common Rust achieved recall values of 99.00&amp;amp;plusmn;0.15% and 98&amp;amp;plusmn;0.39%, respectively. The branch-level and fused-model results support the complementary role of the three spatial representations under the evaluated experimental conditions. The proposed CMF architecture provides an explicit, multiscale learning framework for image-based maize leaf disease classification and motivates further evaluation under practical agricultural conditions.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Explicit Multi-Scale Feature Learning Through a Human Visual System-Inspired Coarse&amp;amp;ndash;Medium&amp;amp;ndash;Fine Network for Maize Leaf Disease Classification</dc:title>
