        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/399">

	<title>AI, Vol. 7, Pages 399: Performance of Stateful Hash-Based PQ-Digital Signatures in Industrial Scenarios</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/399</link>
	<description>Quantum computers endanger the security of digital signatures as they are generated today, e.g., by cryptographic algorithms like RSA, DSA, and ECDSA, whose security is based on algebraic problems. They have to be replaced by quantum-computer-resistant algorithms, so-called PQ (post-quantum) cryptography. This paper describes the performance results of one type of PQ digital signature embedded in the OPC UA communication protocol, measured in a typical industrial environment, demonstrating the communication between a sensor or actuator and an operator over a 5G campus network. The PQ digital signatures tested follow the stateful hash-based digital signature algorithm (SFH-DSA) as defined by ISO/IEC 14888-4. The tests show that this example of quantum-computer-resistant cryptographic algorithms is useful for practical use cases in industrial applications, which are characterized by real-time requirements and reliable communication between electronic components. Computation times, transmission times including overhead, lengths of digital signatures, memory requirements, and other characteristics are measured for different security levels and different types of hardware. These PQ-resistant digital signatures show very good performance properties, which make them useful for future industrial applications.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-03</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>AI, Vol. 7, Pages 399: Performance of Stateful Hash-Based PQ-Digital Signatures in Industrial Scenarios</b></p>
	<p>AI <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2673-2688/7/10/399">doi: 10.3390/ai7100399</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Christoph Ruland
		Anugraha Venkatraman
		Nataša Živić
		</p>
	<p>Quantum computers endanger the security of digital signatures as they are generated today, e.g., by cryptographic algorithms like RSA, DSA, and ECDSA, whose security is based on algebraic problems. They have to be replaced by quantum-computer-resistant algorithms, so-called PQ (post-quantum) cryptography. This paper describes the performance results of one type of PQ digital signature embedded in the OPC UA communication protocol, measured in a typical industrial environment, demonstrating the communication between a sensor or actuator and an operator over a 5G campus network. The PQ digital signatures tested follow the stateful hash-based digital signature algorithm (SFH-DSA) as defined by ISO/IEC 14888-4. The tests show that this example of quantum-computer-resistant cryptographic algorithms is useful for practical use cases in industrial applications, which are characterized by real-time requirements and reliable communication between electronic components. Computation times, transmission times including overhead, lengths of digital signatures, memory requirements, and other characteristics are measured for different security levels and different types of hardware. These PQ-resistant digital signatures show very good performance properties, which make them useful for future industrial applications.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Performance of Stateful Hash-Based PQ-Digital Signatures in Industrial Scenarios</dc:title>
